//@version=6
// PropQuantX Halyard MNQ 15min FREE
//
// Single-candle opening-range breakout with pullback entries and an optional
// loser reversal. Built for MNQ on 15-minute bars, extended hours.
//
// The range is one 15-minute candle. The first bar that CLOSES beyond it takes
// the day's trade and consumes the day whichever side it fired on — there is no
// second attempt if the filters reject it. Stop sits on the opposite edge of the
// range. Flat before the session rolls; nothing is ever carried overnight.
//
// ENTRY MODEL (Entry Type) — the break ARMS the trade, it no longer buys it:
//   Limit Pullback  (default) two limits rest INSIDE the range once the 15m
//                   candle closes beyond it — unit 1 at 25% of the range back
//                   from the broken edge, unit 2 averaging down at 50%. A break
//                   that never retraces is a no-fill day. The $ risk budget is
//                   SPLIT across the two units.
//   Market on Close the original behaviour: one unit at the breakout close.
//
// TARGET (Target) is a PRICE LEVEL projected off the range, not a multiple of the
// stop distance. Long -> orbHigh + pct x range, short -> orbLow - pct x range, at
// 10 / 30 / 50 / 100%; or HOD/LOD, the session extreme as it stood when the setup
// armed. One level serves both units, so an average-down that improves the
// blended entry simply earns more R against the same target.
//
// ONCE THE FIRST TRADE CLOSES the day can get ONE more setup: the REVERSAL,
// opposite to the first trade, on a close beyond the FAR edge. It now arms on
// either outcome — after a stop-out (a failed break fading) and after a target
// (a move that already paid and has since come all the way back through the
// other side). "Reverse Trade After" narrows it to losses only.
//
// Editable: Position Sizing, Trade Management, Day Filter, Range Filter and
// Trade Visualization. Everything else is fixed in the CONFIGURATION block.
//
// Timezone is hardcoded — do not change your chart's timezone expecting the
// levels to move, they will not.
strategy("PropQuantX Halyard MNQ 15min FREE", overlay = true, pyramiding = 2, process_orders_on_close = true, initial_capital = 150000, default_qty_type = strategy.fixed, default_qty_value = 1, slippage = 0, commission_type = strategy.commission.cash_per_contract, commission_value = 0, margin_long = 1, margin_short = 1, max_labels_count = 500, max_boxes_count = 500)

// === Inputs ===
grpPS = "Position Sizing"
useRiskSizing = input.bool(true, "Use $ Risk Position Sizing", group = grpPS)
riskUSD = input.float(535, "Risk Per Trade ($)", minval = 0, group = grpPS)
qtyContracts = input.int(1, "Contracts (If Risk Sizing Off)", minval = 1, group = grpPS)
maxContracts = input.int(1000, "Max Contracts", minval = 1, group = grpPS, tooltip = "Hard cap on the risk-sized quantity.")

// Trade management. The target is a LEVEL projected off the range — there is no
// Risk:Reward input any more, because the target no longer has anything to do
// with the stop distance. What R a trade is worth now falls out of where it
// filled: the same level pays more R from a deeper average-down fill.
grpTM = "Trade Management"
tpMode = input.string("Ext 30%", "Target", options = ["Ext 10%", "Ext 30%", "Ext 50%", "Ext 100%", "HOD/LOD"], group = grpTM, tooltip = "Range extension beyond the edge that broke — long: orbHigh + pct x range, short: orbLow - pct x range. Ext 100% projects the whole range again. HOD/LOD targets the session high (long) or low (short) FROZEN as it stood when the setup armed.")
enableReverseTrade = input.bool(true, "Enable Reverse Trade", group = grpTM, tooltip = "A second setup, opposite to the first trade, on a close beyond the far edge. Raises the day's SETUP cap to 2.")
reverseAfter = input.string("Loss or Target", "Reverse Trade After", options = ["Loss or Target", "Loss Only"], group = grpTM, tooltip = "Loss or Target: the first trade CLOSING arms the reversal, whether it stopped out or hit its target. Loss Only is the older rule — a winning day was finished, and a close back beyond the far edge was left alone.")

// Entry model. The strategy shipped as a MARKET fill on the breakout bar's close;
// Limit Pullback is the alternative under evaluation. Both are implemented once,
// in the single PLACEMENT block far below — see the long note there.
grpE = "Entry"
entryMode = input.string("Limit Pullback", "Entry Type", options = ["Limit Pullback", "Market on Close"], group = grpE, tooltip = "Limit Pullback buys nothing at the break itself: limits rest INSIDE the range, and a break that never retraces is a no-fill day.")
pb1Pct = input.float(25, "Unit 1 — Pullback % Into Range", minval = 1, maxval = 95, step = 1, group = grpE, tooltip = "Measured back from the edge that broke. 25 = a quarter of the range back inside it.")
useAvgDown = input.bool(true, "Average Down With Unit 2", group = grpE, tooltip = "Rests a second same-side limit deeper in the range. The $ risk budget is SPLIT across the two units, not doubled.")
pb2Pct = input.float(50, "Unit 2 — Pullback % Into Range", minval = 1, maxval = 95, step = 1, group = grpE, tooltip = "Only read while Average Down With Unit 2 is on. 50 = the range midpoint.")

// Unit 2 at or above unit 1 is not an average-down: at equal depth both limits
// sit on one price and just double the size, and shallower means unit 2 fills
// FIRST and unit 1 becomes the add. Fail loudly rather than backtest that.
if useAvgDown and entryMode == "Limit Pullback" and pb2Pct <= pb1Pct
    runtime.error("Entry: Unit 2 pullback % must be DEEPER than Unit 1 — it is the average-down, so it has to sit further inside the range.")

// Day filter — one direction setting per weekday. "Off" is a day that never trades.
grpD = "Day Filter"
dayTip = "Read on the SESSION's day, which ends 13:30 / 14:30 ET — not on the calendar day."
dayMon = input.string("Long", "Monday", options = ["Both", "Long", "Short", "Off"], group = grpD, tooltip = dayTip)
dayTue = input.string("Both", "Tuesday", options = ["Both", "Long", "Short", "Off"], group = grpD)
dayWed = input.string("Both", "Wednesday", options = ["Both", "Long", "Short", "Off"], group = grpD)
dayThu = input.string("Both", "Thursday", options = ["Both", "Long", "Short", "Off"], group = grpD)
dayFri = input.string("Both", "Friday", options = ["Both", "Long", "Short", "Off"], group = grpD)

// Range filter — a DAY gate on the range candle's own size, in POINTS. 0 = off on
// either side. BOTH SHIP AT 0, so this changes nothing until a value is set.
grpR = "Range Filter"
orbRangeMinPts = input.float(0, "Min ORB Range (pts, 0=off)", minval = 0, step = 0.25, group = grpR, tooltip = "Skip the day if the range is narrower than this.")
orbRangeMaxPts = input.float(0, "Max ORB Range (pts, 0=off)", minval = 0, step = 0.25, group = grpR, tooltip = "Skip the day if the range is wider than this.")

// Min above Max passes on NOTHING, and a strategy that silently stops trading
// reads as "no signals today" rather than as a typo. Fail loudly instead.
if orbRangeMaxPts > 0 and orbRangeMinPts > orbRangeMaxPts
    runtime.error("Range Filter: Min ORB Range is above Max ORB Range, so no day can ever pass. Fix one of them.")

// Trade visualization. The boxes and their three price labels draw the exact
// stop and target of the live trade.
grpV = "Trade Visualization"
showPnLBoxes = input.bool(true, "Show TP/SL P&L Boxes", group = grpV)
showPnLLabels = input.bool(true, "Show TP/SL Labels", group = grpV)
profitBoxColor = input.color(color.blue, "Profit Box Color", group = grpV)
lossBoxColor = input.color(color.red, "Loss Box Color", group = grpV)

// === CONFIGURATION ===
// Fixed settings. Values marked UNREAD belong to a mode that is not selected —
// they are kept so the mode ternaries below still compile, and a constant
// condition folds anyway.
//
// NOTE WHICH VALUES ARE LIVE: the target is set entirely by the Target input
// above — the fixed-tick and ATR target modes are gone with the R:R model, so
// nothing down here touches the take-profit any more.
tradeDirection = "Both"       // blanket override on top of the per-day grid

slType = "ORB Edge + Buffer"
slBufferPts = 0.0             // ZERO -> the stop sits exactly ON the opposite ORB edge
slFixedTicks = 340            // UNREAD under ORB Edge + Buffer
atrSlMult = 3.8               // UNREAD under ORB Edge + Buffer
// UNREAD under ORB Edge + Buffer. Set slType = "Breakout Candle" above to arm
// the fourth mode: the stop goes beyond the breakout candle instead of the far
// ORB edge, and slBufferPts is then measured past THAT extreme. 1 = the
// breakout candle alone; 2 or 3 also take in the 1-2 candles BEFORE it, and
// since the level is the lowest low / highest high of that window, raising it
// can only widen the stop, never tighten it.
slCandleBars = 1
atrLen = 32                   // UNREAD: no ATR stop mode is picked and the vol filter is gone
maxBarsInTrade = 0            // 0 = TIME STOP OFF. Only TP, SL and the day flat close a trade

// OFF, so there are no entry filters at all: the break, the weekday grid and the
// direction are the whole entry condition. Set true to arm the trend filter.
useShortTrend = false
shortTrendEma = 190

// === ORB settings — the clock is HARDCODED to New York ===
// There is no timezone input any more, and the chart's own timezone setting
// moves nothing: every clock read below passes `tz` explicitly.
//
// THE CONVERSION IS NOT A FIXED HOUR, which is the whole subtlety. The range
// candle is anchored to 10:30 Asia/Kolkata = 05:00 UTC every day of the year,
// because India has no DST and the US does. On the New York clock that is 00:00
// under EST and 01:00 under EDT, so the ET hour has to FOLLOW the US changeover
// or it sits on the wrong bars for half the year. Both seasonal values below are
// therefore derived per bar rather than typed in.
tz = "America/New_York"

// ET's UTC offset in minutes on bar `t`: 300 under EST, 240 under EDT. Read off
// the tape rather than assumed, because the entire point is that it moves. The
// `+ 1440 % 1440` is there because New York and UTC are usually on different
// calendar days, which sends the raw difference negative.
f_etOffsetMin(t) =>
    etM = hour(t, tz) * 60 + minute(t, tz)
    (hour(t, "Etc/UTC") * 60 + minute(t, "Etc/UTC") - etM + 1440) % 1440

// 10:30 Asia/Kolkata written on the New York clock: 00:00 EST / 01:00 EDT.
f_orbStartMin(t) => f_etOffsetMin(t) == 240 ? 60 : 0

// 00:00 Asia/Kolkata — the boundary the trading day rolls on — written on the
// same clock: 13:30 EST / 14:30 EDT. It resets the day's state, picks the
// day-of-week and force-flattens.
f_sessionEndMin(t) => f_etOffsetMin(t) == 240 ? 870 : 810

// The instant this bar's session ENDS, on the New York clock. Shifting a bar
// forward to its own boundary makes the session's date and weekday simply the ET
// date and weekday of the shifted instant — no wrap-around arithmetic anywhere,
// and the key and the day filter cannot drift apart because they read the same
// shift. Bumping a YYYYMMDD key by 1 instead LOOKS equivalent and is not:
// 20260531 + 1 is not 20260601, so every month boundary would split one session
// into two, resetting the day's state and force-flattening mid-session.
f_sessionShift(t) => t + (1440 - f_sessionEndMin(t)) * 60000

f_sessionKey(t) =>
    s = f_sessionShift(t)
    year(s, tz) * 10000 + month(s, tz) * 100 + dayofmonth(s, tz)

// A STRING, not an int: request.security demands a SIMPLE string timeframe, and
// str.tostring() of an int is series-qualified — that combination does not
// compile. `orbMinutes` is gone with the volatility filter, which was the only
// thing that needed the width as a number.
orbTf = "15"

// The two Trade Visualization knobs that stayed locked. The switches and the two
// colours are inputs, up in the Inputs block.
keepTradeBoxes = 20
boxTransp = 70

// The day's SETUP cap is DERIVED from the reversal switch rather than being a
// second, disagreeable input: 2 on, 1 off.
// A SETUP, not a fill: under Limit Pullback one setup rests two limit orders and
// can fill twice (unit 1 plus the average-down). The cap counts breaks acted on.
maxTradesPerDay = enableReverseTrade ? 2 : 1

// === Risk / Position sizing ===
// Quantity is auto-calculated from: riskUSD / (abs(entry - stop) * syminfo.pointvalue)
calcQty(risk, entry, sl) =>
    riskPerPoint = math.abs(entry - sl)
    riskPerContract = riskPerPoint * syminfo.pointvalue
    math.max(math.floor(risk / (riskPerContract > 0 ? riskPerContract : syminfo.mintick * syminfo.pointvalue)), 1)

// `_budget` is the $ risk THIS unit may spend, not the day's whole allowance:
// the average-down splits riskUSD across the units it rests. With risk sizing
// OFF the split does not apply — qtyContracts is a per-unit count, so two units
// mean twice the contracts.
f_finalQty(_entry, _sl, _budget) =>
    q = useRiskSizing ? calcQty(_budget, _entry, _sl) : qtyContracts
    math.min(q, maxContracts)

// === TP/SL P&L visualization (boxes + labels) ===
// A BLUE profit box from entry to target, a RED risk box from entry to stop, and
// three text-only price labels (target / entry / stop) anchored at the entry bar
// with fully transparent backgrounds.
var box curRiskBox = na
var box curProfitBox = na
var label curSLLabel = na
var label curTPLabel = na
var label curEntryLabel = na

var array<box> histBoxes = array.new<box>()
var array<label> histLabels = array.new<label>()

// Extend the boxes to the right while the trade is live. The labels stay put at
// the entry bar, which keeps the three prices readable against the box edges
// instead of sliding along with price.
// math.max against left+8, never a bare bar_index: the boxes are BORN 8 bars
// wide, so assigning bar_index would shrink them back on the very next bar.
if showPnLBoxes and strategy.position_size != 0
    if not na(curRiskBox)
        box.set_right(curRiskBox, math.max(box.get_left(curRiskBox) + 8, bar_index))
    if not na(curProfitBox)
        box.set_right(curProfitBox, math.max(box.get_left(curProfitBox) + 8, bar_index))

// When a position closes, either keep or delete the boxes/labels based on keepTradeBoxes
posJustClosed = bar_index >= 1 and strategy.position_size == 0 and strategy.position_size[1] != 0
if showPnLBoxes and posJustClosed
    // Minimum 8 bars wide — a trade that closes in one or two bars
    // would otherwise leave a sliver too thin to read.
    if not na(curRiskBox)
        box.set_right(curRiskBox, math.max(box.get_left(curRiskBox) + 8, bar_index))
    if not na(curProfitBox)
        box.set_right(curProfitBox, math.max(box.get_left(curProfitBox) + 8, bar_index))

    if keepTradeBoxes == 0
        if not na(curRiskBox)
            box.delete(curRiskBox)
        if not na(curProfitBox)
            box.delete(curProfitBox)
        if not na(curSLLabel)
            label.delete(curSLLabel)
        if not na(curTPLabel)
            label.delete(curTPLabel)
        if not na(curEntryLabel)
            label.delete(curEntryLabel)
    else
        if not na(curRiskBox)
            array.push(histBoxes, curRiskBox)
        if not na(curProfitBox)
            array.push(histBoxes, curProfitBox)
        if not na(curSLLabel)
            array.push(histLabels, curSLLabel)
        if not na(curTPLabel)
            array.push(histLabels, curTPLabel)
        if not na(curEntryLabel)
            array.push(histLabels, curEntryLabel)

    curRiskBox := na
    curProfitBox := na
    curSLLabel := na
    curTPLabel := na
    curEntryLabel := na

// Enforce history limit on every bar (also handles reducing keepTradeBoxes)
if showPnLBoxes
    maxBoxCount = keepTradeBoxes * 2
    maxLabelCount = keepTradeBoxes * 3

    while keepTradeBoxes >= 0 and array.size(histBoxes) > maxBoxCount
        oldB = array.shift(histBoxes)
        box.delete(oldB)

    while keepTradeBoxes >= 0 and array.size(histLabels) > maxLabelCount
        oldL = array.shift(histLabels)
        label.delete(oldL)

// === Visual defaults (kept as constants, not inputs) ===
// The breakout TRIANGLE marks the bar that BREAKS the range, not the range
// candle itself.
showBreakoutMarker = true
orbHighColor = color.new(color.green, 30)
orbLowColor = color.new(color.red, 30)

// === Helpers (evaluated on the ORB timeframe inside request.security) ===
// `time` here is the ORB-timeframe bar's own time, so the DST test resolves on
// that bar rather than on the chart bar.
f_isOrbCandle() =>
    hour(time, tz) * 60 + minute(time, tz) == f_orbStartMin(time)

f_orbHighConfirmed() =>
    isOrb = f_isOrbCandle()
    isOrb and barstate.isconfirmed ? high : na

f_orbLowConfirmed() =>
    isOrb = f_isOrbCandle()
    isOrb and barstate.isconfirmed ? low : na

f_orbStartTimeConfirmed() =>
    isOrb = f_isOrbCandle()
    isOrb and barstate.isconfirmed ? time : na

// === Get the ORB candle High/Low (timezone anchored, timeframe anchored) ===
orbHighNew = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, orbTf, f_orbHighConfirmed(), gaps = barmerge.gaps_off, lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
orbLowNew = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, orbTf, f_orbLowConfirmed(), gaps = barmerge.gaps_off, lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
orbStartNew = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, orbTf, f_orbStartTimeConfirmed(), gaps = barmerge.gaps_off, lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)

// Reset once per session, on the 13:30 / 14:30 ET boundary — NOT on ET midnight.
sessionKey = f_sessionKey(time)
newDay = ta.change(sessionKey)

// === Persistent state ===
var float orbHigh = na
var float orbLow = na
var int orbStartTime = na

// The day's first break latches here and consumes the day whichever side it
// fired on.
var bool breakoutMarked = false

// Daily reset
if bool(newDay)
    orbHigh := na
    orbLow := na
    orbStartTime := na
    breakoutMarked := false

// When the ORB candle closes (confirmed), store its high/low as the day's levels.
orbReadyNow = not na(orbHighNew) and not na(orbLowNew) and not na(orbStartNew)
orbTrigger = orbReadyNow and (na(orbStartTime) or orbStartNew != orbStartTime)
if orbTrigger
    orbHigh := orbHighNew
    orbLow := orbLowNew
    orbStartTime := orbStartNew
    breakoutMarked := false

levelsAvailable = not na(orbHigh) and not na(orbLow) and not na(orbStartTime)

// === Session high / low — the HOD/LOD target reference ===
// Session-scoped, on the same 13:30 / 14:30 ET boundary as everything else, NOT
// the calendar day: an extreme that carried across the roll would be pointing at
// a range the current session never traded.
// These keep RUNNING; the setup freezes a copy of them at arm time. A target that
// chased the session high instead could never be reached by a long — it would sit
// at or above price on every bar by construction.
var float dHigh = na
var float dLow = na
if bool(newDay) or na(dHigh) or na(dLow)
    dHigh := high
    dLow := low
else
    dHigh := math.max(dHigh, high)
    dLow := math.min(dLow, low)

// === Indicators ===
// The ATR is only referenced by mode branches that are not selected.
emaShortVal = ta.ema(close, shortTrendEma)
atrVal = ta.atr(atrLen)

// === Entry filters ===
// RANGE is a DAY gate: it gates the BREAKOUT LATCH below, not the entry. That
// distinction is the whole behaviour — a day the range rejects is skipped ENTIRELY
// (no first trade, no reversal, no triangle drawn) and is NOT
// consumed, because `breakoutMarked` can only latch on a day allowed to break out
// in the first place.
//
// Knowable at the bars it gates: the range is fixed when the ORB candle closes,
// which is before anything can trade that day.
orbRange = orbHigh - orbLow
rangeDayOk = not na(orbRange) and (orbRangeMinPts <= 0 or orbRange >= orbRangeMinPts) and (orbRangeMaxPts <= 0 or orbRange <= orbRangeMaxPts)

// The per-BAR filter, and it is SHORTS ONLY. It ships off. Unlike the range gate
// above, a bar filter rejecting a break still CONSUMES the day — the latch fires
// on the break, not on the entry.
filterShortOK = not useShortTrend or (not na(emaShortVal) and close < emaShortVal)

// `breakoutMarked` latches the day's first break, so anything after it is ignored.
breakoutUp = levelsAvailable and rangeDayOk and not breakoutMarked and barstate.isconfirmed and close > orbHigh
breakoutDn = levelsAvailable and rangeDayOk and not breakoutMarked and barstate.isconfirmed and close < orbLow
breakoutClose = breakoutUp or breakoutDn

// The first break consumes the day whichever side it fires on, and whether or
// not it passes its filters, so a filter can only ever REMOVE a trade, never
// substitute a later one.
if breakoutClose
    breakoutMarked := true

plotshape(showBreakoutMarker and breakoutUp, title = "Close Above ORB High", style = shape.triangleup, location = location.abovebar, color = orbHighColor, size = size.tiny)
plotshape(showBreakoutMarker and breakoutDn, title = "Close Below ORB Low", style = shape.triangledown, location = location.belowbar, color = orbLowColor, size = size.tiny)

// === Stop / target by type ===
// ORB Edge + Buffer and Breakout Candle are structural (a price level, pushed
// slBufferPts PAST it). The other two are distances from the entry price.
//
// The two window extremes are computed HERE, at global scope, on every bar —
// NOT inside f_longStop/f_shortStop. Those are called from inside the
// PLACEMENT block, and a ta.* call that only runs on the bars where a setup
// arms does not see the continuous history it needs: it would return a
// value taken over whatever sparse set of bars happened to trigger, which on a
// once-a-day strategy is days apart. `ta.lowest(low, n)` INCLUDES the current
// bar, so the window ends at the breakout candle itself with no offset — and
// that is correct rather than lookahead, because Pine fills the entry at the
// NEXT bar's open, by which point this bar has closed.
//
// NOTE: no session reset. At slCandleBars > 1 a breakout within 1-2 bars of the
// session start reaches into the prior session. Only reachable when the range
// candle sits right at the open.
slWinLow = ta.lowest(low, slCandleBars)
slWinHigh = ta.highest(high, slCandleBars)
// Floored at one tick. slBufferPts ships at 0.0, and a
// breakout candle that closes on its own low puts the raw level exactly ON
// `close` — which is the entry reference calcQty divides by, so the risk per
// contract would be zero and the size would slam into maxContracts. The floor
// is what stops that, so do not drop it when tuning the buffer to 0.
// NOTE the unit: slBufferPts is POINTS, not ticks. On MNQ/NQ one tick is 0.25
// points, so one tick of buffer is 0.25 here, not 1.
slCandleBuf = math.max(slBufferPts, syminfo.mintick)

f_longStop(entryPx) =>
    slType == "ORB Edge + Buffer" ? orbLow - slBufferPts : slType == "Breakout Candle" ? slWinLow - slCandleBuf : slType == "Fixed Ticks" ? entryPx - slFixedTicks * syminfo.mintick : entryPx - atrVal * atrSlMult

f_shortStop(entryPx) =>
    slType == "ORB Edge + Buffer" ? orbHigh + slBufferPts : slType == "Breakout Candle" ? slWinHigh + slCandleBuf : slType == "Fixed Ticks" ? entryPx + slFixedTicks * syminfo.mintick : entryPx + atrVal * atrSlMult

// === Target — a LEVEL projected off the range, not a multiple of the stop ===
// The extensions are measured from the edge that BROKE, mirroring how the entry
// percentages are measured: long -> orbHigh + pct x range, short -> orbLow - pct
// x range. Ext 100% projects the whole range again beyond the edge.
//
// Because the level is fixed and the entry is not, R is now an OUTPUT rather than
// an input: unit 1 and an averaged-down position aim at the same price, and the
// deeper blended entry simply collects more of the distance.
//
// HOD/LOD takes the session extreme as it stood when the setup armed. It is built
// for the pullback model, where the entry sits back inside the range and the
// extreme the breakout push already made is a real distance above it. Under
// Market on Close it very nearly degenerates — the entry IS the breakout bar's
// close, so the session high is usually that same bar's high, a few ticks away.
f_tpLevel(_dir, _dHi, _dLo) =>
    ext = tpMode == "Ext 10%" ? 0.10 : tpMode == "Ext 30%" ? 0.30 : tpMode == "Ext 50%" ? 0.50 : tpMode == "Ext 100%" ? 1.00 : 0.0
    _dir == 1 ? (tpMode == "HOD/LOD" ? _dHi : orbHigh + orbRange * ext) : (tpMode == "HOD/LOD" ? _dLo : orbLow - orbRange * ext)

// === Strategy Execution ===
// Day filter (uses ORB timezone)
// The SESSION's day-of-week, not the bar's: a bar past the 13:30 / 14:30 ET
// boundary already belongs to tomorrow's session. Read off the same shifted
// instant as the session key, so the two agree by construction.
dow = dayofweek(f_sessionShift(time), tz)
// Saturday and Sunday fall through to "Off", which is what the five booleans did
// before — nothing here has to special-case the weekend.
dayMode = dow == dayofweek.monday ? dayMon : dow == dayofweek.tuesday ? dayTue : dow == dayofweek.wednesday ? dayWed : dow == dayofweek.thursday ? dayThu : dow == dayofweek.friday ? dayFri : "Off"
tradeDayOK = dayMode != "Off"

// The per-day setting ANDs into the global Trade Direction rather than replacing
// it, so the global still works as a blanket override on top of the per-day grid.
directionLongOK = (tradeDirection == "Both" or tradeDirection == "Long") and (dayMode == "Both" or dayMode == "Long")
directionShortOK = (tradeDirection == "Both" or tradeDirection == "Short") and (dayMode == "Both" or dayMode == "Short")

// State machine (per day):
// 0 = no trade yet (wait for first breakout)
// 1 = first trade placed (wait for it to close)
// 2 = first trade CLOSED and the day is still open (wait for the OPPOSITE-side
//     close beyond the ORB that arms the reversal). Reached after a stop-out
//     always, and after a target too under Reverse Trade After = Loss or Target
// 3 = done for the day (the reversal was taken, or a green first trade under
//     Loss Only)
//
// State 2 is the only state the reversal can arm from and it leaves state 3, so
// the day is capped at two SETUPS and the reversal is never itself reversed.
var int tradeState = 0
var int firstTradeDir = 0
var bool firstWasLoss = false  // how the first trade ended, for the reversal's label
var int prevClosedTrades = 0
var int tradesToday = 0

if bool(newDay)
    tradeState := 0
    firstTradeDir := 0
    firstWasLoss := false
    tradesToday := 0
    prevClosedTrades := strategy.closedtrades

// Detect when a setup closes and whether it ended red or green.
// SUMMED over every trade closed since the last check, not read off the last one:
// an averaged-down setup closes TWO units, usually on the same bar, and unit 2
// (filled deeper, so further from the target) can end red while the setup as a
// whole ends green. The blended result is the one that decides the day.
//
// WHICH OUTCOMES LEAVE THE DAY OPEN is the Reverse Trade After setting. Under
// "Loss or Target" the first trade simply CLOSING hands the day to state 2 and
// the reversal can arm on either outcome — a target hit no longer ends the
// session, so a move that runs to the extension and then rolls all the way back
// through the opposite edge is tradeable. Under "Loss Only" a green first trade
// goes straight to 3, which is how the strategy behaved before.
// The outcome is still recorded, because it changes what the reversal IS: after a
// loss it is a failed-break fade, after a target it is a genuine reversal of a
// move that already paid. They are worth telling apart in the trade list.
//
// Guarded on tradeState == 1, so the second setup closing cannot walk the state
// backwards out of 3 — the day stays done however that trade ends.
if strategy.closedtrades > prevClosedTrades
    float setupProfit = 0.0
    for i = prevClosedTrades to strategy.closedtrades - 1
        setupProfit += strategy.closedtrades.profit(i)
    if tradeState == 1
        firstWasLoss := setupProfit < 0
        tradeState := (firstWasLoss or reverseAfter == "Loss or Target") ? 2 : 3
    prevClosedTrades := strategy.closedtrades

// === ARM — which setup fired, and on which side ===
// The two setups decide DIRECTION and label ONLY. Neither sends an order: the
// single PLACEMENT block below owns every order, which is why Market on Close and
// Limit Pullback are each written once instead of four times.
int armDir = 0                 // 1 = long setup armed this bar, -1 = short, 0 = none
string armTag = ""             // shows up in the Strategy Tester's List of Trades

// --- First trade (take the FIRST breakout triangle, if it passes the filters) ---
enterLong = breakoutUp and directionLongOK and tradeDayOK and tradesToday < maxTradesPerDay and strategy.position_size == 0 and tradeState == 0
if enterLong
    firstTradeDir := 1
    tradeState := 1
    tradesToday += 1
    armDir := 1
    armTag := "First"

enterShort = breakoutDn and directionShortOK and tradeDayOK and filterShortOK and tradesToday < maxTradesPerDay and strategy.position_size == 0 and tradeState == 0
if enterShort
    firstTradeDir := -1
    tradeState := 1
    tradesToday += 1
    armDir := -1
    armTag := "First"

// --- Reversal trade (the day's second setup, opposite to the first) ---
// The ONLY second chance the day has. There is no same-direction re-entry: a
// close back beyond the SAME edge re-arms nothing, so the day's remaining trade
// is this one or none.
//
// TWO WAYS IN, and which are live is Reverse Trade After:
//   after a LOSS   — the break failed, was stopped on the far edge, and price is
//                    now closing beyond that far edge. A failed-break fade.
//   after a TARGET — the break paid out at its extension and price has since come
//                    all the way back through the opposite edge. The move is done
//                    and turning, and the day is not over just because it won.
// Both land in state 2 and both read the same trigger below; only the label
// differs. "Loss Only" narrows it back to the first case.
//
// NOT filtered — gated on the day-of-week, the direction and the closed first
// trade only. The entry filters apply to the FIRST trade.
//
// Note the per-day direction grid DOES reach it, via directionLongOK /
// directionShortOK: a weekday set to one side can never take the reversal, since
// the reversal is by definition the opposite side of the losing first trade.
//
// `breakoutUp` / `breakoutDn` are already spent for the day — `breakoutMarked`
// latched on the first break — so the level test is written out directly here.
revLong = enableReverseTrade and tradeState == 2 and firstTradeDir == -1 and directionLongOK and tradeDayOK and tradesToday < maxTradesPerDay and strategy.position_size == 0 and barstate.isconfirmed and levelsAvailable and close > orbHigh
if revLong
    tradeState := 3
    tradesToday += 1
    armDir := 1
    armTag := firstWasLoss ? "Reversal (loss)" : "Reversal (target)"

revShort = enableReverseTrade and tradeState == 2 and firstTradeDir == 1 and directionShortOK and tradeDayOK and tradesToday < maxTradesPerDay and strategy.position_size == 0 and barstate.isconfirmed and levelsAvailable and close < orbLow
if revShort
    tradeState := 3
    tradesToday += 1
    armDir := -1
    armTag := firstWasLoss ? "Reversal (loss)" : "Reversal (target)"

// === The armed setup's working state ===
// These outlive the arm bar because a resting limit can sit unfilled for hours,
// and the exits need the stop and the target long after the break that set them.
var int pbDir = 0              // side that owns the resting orders / open units
var float pbStop = na          // the ONE stop both units share
var float pbTarget = na        // the ONE target both units share
var float pbE1 = na            // unit 1 limit (na in Market on Close)
var float pbE2 = na            // unit 2 limit (na unless averaging down)
var bool pbResting = false     // true while an unfilled limit is still working

// === CANCEL — a resting limit must never outlive its setup ===
// Runs BEFORE placement, deliberately: a reversal can arm on the very bar the
// first setup's close is detected, and cancelling after placement would kill the
// orders it just sent.
//
// TWO triggers, and only two. The position closing is the one that matters:
// unfilled unit 2 left working after its setup has closed would open a brand new
// position on a day whose trade is already over. The session roll is the sweep.
//
// NOT "the day is done" — a setup that has ARMED but not yet filled is flat with
// its state already advanced, and cancelling on that would delete the reversal's
// own limits one bar after placing them. Unfilled limits are supposed to rest for
// as long as the session lasts; the day flat at the bottom is what ends them.
if pbResting and (bool(newDay) or posJustClosed)
    strategy.cancel("L1")
    strategy.cancel("L2")
    strategy.cancel("S1")
    strategy.cancel("S2")
    pbResting := false
    pbE1 := na
    pbE2 := na

// === PLACEMENT — the only place orders are sent ===
// Two entry models:
//
//   Market on Close — the original. ONE unit at the breakout bar's close, which
//                     is also the fill price under process_orders_on_close.
//
//   Limit Pullback  — nothing is bought at the break. Two limits rest INSIDE the
//                     range and the trade only exists if price comes back for it:
//                     unit 1 at pb1Pct of the range back from the broken edge,
//                     unit 2 (the average-down) at pb2Pct. A break that runs away
//                     without retracing is a no-fill day — that is the trade-off
//                     being measured, better fills against fewer of them.
//
// The entry percentages are measured from the EDGE THAT BROKE, so the number
// means the same thing on both sides: how far back into the range price must
// retrace before the limit is touched. Long -> orbHigh - pct * range. Short ->
// orbLow + pct * range. The target is projected off the same edge, outward.
//
// The stop is the SAME structural level in both models — the far ORB edge — so a
// pullback fill sits CLOSER to it than a market fill does (0.75 x range at the 25%
// line, against the full close-to-far-edge distance), the risk per contract is
// smaller, and $ risk sizing hands back MORE contracts for the same dollars.
//
// Both the stop and the target are frozen HERE, at arm time, and never move
// again. That is what lets the average-down be worth something: two units, one
// stop, one target, and a blended entry that is closer to the target than unit 1
// alone was.
//
// CAVEAT for the two distance stop modes (Fixed Ticks / ATR): they measure from
// the price handed to them, and at placement the fill price is not known yet, so
// they measure from the BREAKOUT CLOSE while the real fill lands inside it. Both
// are UNREAD in the shipped config (slType is ORB Edge + Buffer, a level), so
// this only matters to someone who switches slType.
if armDir != 0
    // Clear the board first. The entries below reuse their own IDs, so those are
    // replaced automatically — but a reversal is by definition the OPPOSITE side,
    // and the previous setup's S1/S2 (or L1/L2) are not touched by placing L1/L2.
    // A stale limit from the first setup left resting here would fill against the
    // new one and hedge the day into a net-flat mess.
    strategy.cancel("L1")
    strategy.cancel("L2")
    strategy.cancel("S1")
    strategy.cancel("S2")
    stopPx = armDir == 1 ? f_longStop(close) : f_shortStop(close)
    tgtPx = f_tpLevel(armDir, dHigh, dLow)
    pbDir := armDir
    pbStop := stopPx
    if entryMode == "Market on Close"
        mktQty = f_finalQty(close, stopPx, riskUSD)
        // A target at or behind the entry would fill for nothing the instant the
        // unit does. Only HOD/LOD can produce that — the session extreme can sit
        // a tick from a market fill on a bar that closes on its own high — and a
        // tick of clearance is all it takes to keep the order meaningful.
        pbTarget := armDir == 1 ? math.max(tgtPx, close + syminfo.mintick) : math.min(tgtPx, close - syminfo.mintick)
        pbE1 := na
        pbE2 := na
        if armDir == 1
            strategy.entry("L1", strategy.long, qty = mktQty, comment = armTag)
            strategy.exit("L1 TP/SL", from_entry = "L1", stop = stopPx, limit = pbTarget)
        else
            strategy.entry("S1", strategy.short, qty = mktQty, comment = armTag)
            strategy.exit("S1 TP/SL", from_entry = "S1", stop = stopPx, limit = pbTarget)
    else
        e1 = armDir == 1 ? orbHigh - orbRange * pb1Pct / 100.0 : orbLow + orbRange * pb1Pct / 100.0
        e2 = armDir == 1 ? orbHigh - orbRange * pb2Pct / 100.0 : orbLow + orbRange * pb2Pct / 100.0
        // The $ budget is SPLIT across the units that actually rest, so a day
        // where both fill risks riskUSD in total rather than twice it. The cost
        // of holding a second unit in reserve is that a day where only unit 1
        // fills risks HALF the budget — that is the honest accounting, and it is
        // why the average-down is not free upside.
        units = useAvgDown ? 2 : 1
        bud = riskUSD / units
        q1 = f_finalQty(e1, stopPx, bud)
        q2 = f_finalQty(e2, stopPx, bud)
        // Floored against UNIT 1, which is the shallower level and therefore the
        // one nearest the target — clearing it clears the average-down too.
        pbTarget := armDir == 1 ? math.max(tgtPx, e1 + syminfo.mintick) : math.min(tgtPx, e1 - syminfo.mintick)
        pbE1 := e1
        pbE2 := useAvgDown ? e2 : na
        pbResting := true
        if armDir == 1
            strategy.entry("L1", strategy.long, qty = q1, limit = e1, comment = armTag)
            strategy.exit("L1 TP/SL", from_entry = "L1", stop = stopPx, limit = pbTarget)
            if useAvgDown
                strategy.entry("L2", strategy.long, qty = q2, limit = e2, comment = armTag + " avg")
                strategy.exit("L2 TP/SL", from_entry = "L2", stop = stopPx, limit = pbTarget)
        else
            strategy.entry("S1", strategy.short, qty = q1, limit = e1, comment = armTag)
            strategy.exit("S1 TP/SL", from_entry = "S1", stop = stopPx, limit = pbTarget)
            if useAvgDown
                strategy.entry("S2", strategy.short, qty = q2, limit = e2, comment = armTag + " avg")
                strategy.exit("S2 TP/SL", from_entry = "S2", stop = stopPx, limit = pbTarget)

// === EXIT ORDERS — one stop and one target for both units ===
// Both are the LEVELS frozen at arm time, so there is nothing to recompute when
// the average-down fills. Re-issued on every bar a position is open: the exits
// are also attached at placement, so a unit that fills mid-bar is covered on that
// same bar, and this only keeps them alive and current.
if pbDir != 0 and strategy.position_size != 0 and not na(pbStop) and not na(pbTarget)
    strategy.exit("L1 TP/SL", from_entry = "L1", stop = pbStop, limit = pbTarget)
    strategy.exit("L2 TP/SL", from_entry = "L2", stop = pbStop, limit = pbTarget)
    strategy.exit("S1 TP/SL", from_entry = "S1", stop = pbStop, limit = pbTarget)
    strategy.exit("S2 TP/SL", from_entry = "S2", stop = pbStop, limit = pbTarget)

// === RISK / REWARD BOXES — one routine, both entry models ===
// Drawn off the POSITION rather than off the signal, so the boxes show the trade
// that actually exists: nothing is drawn for a break whose limits never fill, and
// an average-down redraws against the new blended entry. The stop and target
// edges do not move — only the entry line between them does, which is exactly
// what the add is for.
// The left edge is the first open unit's own fill bar, so a redraw on the
// average-down bar does not drag the box forward.
var float tradeEntryPx = na
var float tradeStopPx = na
var float tradeTargetPx = na
posSizeChanged = strategy.position_size != nz(strategy.position_size[1])
vizRefresh = posSizeChanged and strategy.position_size != 0 and not na(pbTarget)
vizLeft = strategy.opentrades > 0 ? strategy.opentrades.entry_bar_index(0) : bar_index
if vizRefresh
    tradeEntryPx := strategy.position_avg_price
    tradeStopPx := pbStop
    tradeTargetPx := pbTarget

// A profit box entry->target, a risk box entry->stop, both starting 8 bars wide,
// and three text-only price labels on transparent backgrounds so only the
// numbers show against the boxes.
if vizRefresh and showPnLBoxes
    // clear the previous draw — on an average-down this is the pre-blend pair
    if not na(curRiskBox)
        box.delete(curRiskBox)
    if not na(curProfitBox)
        box.delete(curProfitBox)
    if not na(curSLLabel)
        label.delete(curSLLabel)
    if not na(curTPLabel)
        label.delete(curTPLabel)
    if not na(curEntryLabel)
        label.delete(curEntryLabel)

    curProfitBox := box.new(left = vizLeft, top = math.max(tradeEntryPx, tradeTargetPx), right = math.max(vizLeft + 8, bar_index), bottom = math.min(tradeEntryPx, tradeTargetPx), bgcolor = color.new(profitBoxColor, boxTransp), border_width = 0)
    curRiskBox := box.new(left = vizLeft, top = math.max(tradeEntryPx, tradeStopPx), right = math.max(vizLeft + 8, bar_index), bottom = math.min(tradeEntryPx, tradeStopPx), bgcolor = color.new(lossBoxColor, boxTransp), border_width = 0)

    if showPnLLabels
        curTPLabel := label.new(vizLeft, tradeTargetPx, str.tostring(tradeTargetPx, "#.##"), style = label.style_label_right, color = color.new(profitBoxColor, 100), textcolor = profitBoxColor, size = size.small)
        curEntryLabel := label.new(vizLeft, tradeEntryPx, str.tostring(tradeEntryPx, "#.##"), style = label.style_label_right, color = color.new(color.white, 100), textcolor = color.white, size = size.small)
        curSLLabel := label.new(vizLeft, tradeStopPx, str.tostring(tradeStopPx, "#.##"), style = label.style_label_right, color = color.new(lossBoxColor, 100), textcolor = lossBoxColor, size = size.small)

// The resting limits, drawn only while they are actually working — without them a
// Limit Pullback day shows a breakout triangle and then nothing at all until
// price either comes back or the session ends.
// Unit 1 is the shallower level, so it is always the first one price can reach:
// an open position means it has filled, and its line ends there. Unit 2 keeps
// working THROUGH an open position — that is the average-down — so its line runs
// until the setup closes or the session cancels it.
plot(pbResting and strategy.position_size == 0 ? pbE1 : na, title = "Unit 1 Limit", color = color.new(color.yellow, 20), style = plot.style_linebr, linewidth = 1)
plot(pbResting ? pbE2 : na, title = "Unit 2 Limit (avg down)", color = color.new(color.orange, 20), style = plot.style_linebr, linewidth = 1)

// The reversal arms long after the day's breakout triangle is spent, so it gets
// its own marker — a diamond on the ARM bar, above for a long and below for a
// short. Under Limit Pullback that marks where the setup armed, not where it
// filled; the box is what shows a fill.
plotshape(showBreakoutMarker and revLong, title = "Reversal Long Setup", style = shape.diamond, location = location.abovebar, color = orbHighColor, size = size.tiny)
plotshape(showBreakoutMarker and revShort, title = "Reversal Short Setup", style = shape.diamond, location = location.belowbar, color = orbLowColor, size = size.tiny)


// === DAY HIGHLIGHT ===
// ORANGE = the day is Off. A faint GREEN / RED wash = the day is one-way, so the
// setting is readable off the chart instead of only out of the input dialog.
isWeekday = dow >= dayofweek.monday and dow <= dayofweek.friday
bgcolor(not isWeekday ? na : not tradeDayOK ? color.new(color.orange, 95) : dayMode == "Long" ? color.new(color.green, 96) : dayMode == "Short" ? color.new(color.red, 96) : na)

// === Exits ===
// TIME STOP: flat at the close of the Nth bar after the entry. Counted from the
// FILL bar, so this reads the open trade's own entry bar rather than the signal.
if maxBarsInTrade > 0 and strategy.position_size != 0 and strategy.opentrades > 0
    if bar_index - strategy.opentrades.entry_bar_index(0) >= maxBarsInTrade
        strategy.close_all(comment = "time stop")

// END-OF-DAY FLAT: never carry a position past the session its levels belong to,
// i.e. past 13:30 ET / 14:30 ET. This must fire on the LAST bar of that session;
// a Pine script cannot see the next bar, so it projects one bar-length forward
// instead — exact on every ordinary boundary. `newDay` is the catch-up for
// boundaries that sit inside a market gap, where the projection lands in the gap
// and misses.
//
// KNOWN GAP: that catch-up is NOT "one bar late". On a holiday early close the
// tape halts at 13:00 ET, BEFORE the 13:30/14:30 boundary — the last bar opens
// 12:45 and projects to 13:00, which is still inside the session, so no flat
// fires and `newDay` does not arrive until the next session opens. On those ~10
// sessions a year the position is carried for hours, and over a holiday weekend
// for up to ~77. Watch those dates if you trade this live.
//
// This boundary is load-bearing: letting the position run to ET midnight
// instead leaves the morning's levels armed into the 18:00 ET Globex reopen,
// which is a different session, and the trades that adds lose money.
tfMs = timeframe.in_seconds(timeframe.period) * 1000
sessionEnding = f_sessionKey(time + tfMs) != sessionKey or bool(newDay)
if sessionEnding
    if strategy.position_size != 0
        strategy.close_all(comment = "day flat")
    // A resting limit has to die on the SAME bar the session flattens. The cancel
    // block up in the execution section catches the newDay bar, but that is one
    // bar too late for an order that can fill during it — and a fill into the
    // next session would be working off levels that no longer exist.
    strategy.cancel("L1")
    strategy.cancel("L2")
    strategy.cancel("S1")
    strategy.cancel("S2")
    pbResting := false
